  <details class="why"><summary>Why an approval chain is a declaration</summary>
    
    <p class="note">Every chain below is declared in <code>config/workflows.json</code> &mdash; who
       decides each step, in what order, and the amount at which another approver joins. A customer
       who wants one more signature above ₹50,000 changes a number &mdash; and changes it from
       <b>inside this application</b>, on the screen behind each &ldquo;change the
       thresholds&rdquo; button, not in a file on a server. SmartPRS&nbsp;2
       answers these screens from a 108&nbsp;KB controller where each chain is written out in PHP,
       so the same change is a release.</p>
  </details>
<?php
  // THE PLATFORM'S OWN PUBLISH GATES, RUN AGAINST WHAT IS DECLARED. Surface\Requests
  // walks the chain below linearly and works; Platform\Workflow has a validator
  // with eleven gates and nothing had ever put a declaration through it. A
  // workflow that cannot reach an end node, or whose approval has an outcome no
  // transition covers, is a request that gets raised and then STRANDS — sitting
  // in a queue pointing at a node nothing can leave, with the leave or the money
  // frozen behind it. Catching that when the file is written is worth more than
  // catching it when somebody's request is in it.
  $gateReports = $publishedWorkflows->reports();
  $gateRefused = $publishedWorkflows->unpublishable();
  $gateRefusal = $publishedWorkflows->refusal();
?>
  <div class="card">
    <h2>Publish gates</h2>
<?php if ($gateRefusal !== null): ?>
    <div class="problem"><?= e($gateRefusal) ?></div>
<?php elseif ($gateRefused !== []): ?>
    <div class="problem"><b><?= count($gateRefused) ?> declared chain(s) would strand a request.</b>
      Fix these before anybody raises one.</div>
    <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Chain</th><th>Gate</th><th>What it refuses</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
<?php foreach ($gateRefused as $key): ?>
<?php foreach ($gateReports[$key]->blocking() as $check): ?>
      <tr><td><code><?= e($key) ?></code></td>
          <td><span class="badge badge-red"><?= e($check->gate) ?></span></td>
          <td class="note"><?= e(implode('; ', $check->messages)) ?></td></tr>
<?php endforeach; ?>
<?php endforeach; ?>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    </div>
<?php else: ?>
    <p><span class="badge badge-green"><?= count($gateReports) ?> chain(s) publishable</span>
       Every declared chain compiles into a real workflow definition and passes all eleven of
       <code>Platform\Workflow\Validator</code>'s gates.</p>
    <p class="note">Four of them refused this very file when the compiler was first written, and
       each was right: an approval produces five outcomes and not two (the ones nobody thinks
       about — <code>escalated</code>, <code>returned</code>, <code>auto_decided</code> — are
       exactly what strands a request); a chain whose end is a step is a chain that never ends; an
       approver returning a request to themselves is an infinite loop nobody can see; and a task
       node with no participant can only ever fail the instance. SmartPRS&nbsp;2 has no equivalent
       check at all — a chain there is whatever the controller happens to do.</p>
<?php endif; ?>
  </div>

<?php foreach ($requests->workflows() as $wf): ?>
  <div class="card">
    <h2><?= e((string) $wf['label']) ?>
      <span class="note">on <code><?= e((string) $wf['subject']) ?></code><?php
