<?php
    $one = $reports->find((string) ($_GET['k'] ?? ''));
    // Derived per report, and applied on BOTH the list and the run. A report
    // hidden from the list but still answerable by its URL is a permission check
    // that is really a UI convention.
    $allowed = static function (array $r) use ($reports, $may): array {
        $gate = $reports->permissionsFor($r);
        $denied = array_values(array_filter($gate['permissions'],
            static fn (string $p): bool => !$may($p)));

        return ['ok' => $gate['why'] === null && $denied === [],
            'why' => $gate['why'], 'denied' => $denied, 'needs' => $gate['permissions']];
    };
?>
<?php if ($one === null): ?>
  <div class="page-header"><div><h2>Reports</h2></div></div>
  <details class="why"><summary>Why a report is a row in a file</summary>
    
    <p class="note">Each of these is a row in <code>config/reports.json</code> naming a table, what
       to group by, and what to count or total. A new report is an entry in that file. SmartPRS 2
       answers each report from its own controller, so a change to how a total is formatted has to
       be made in every one of them.</p>
    <p class="note">Aggregation happens <b>in PHP over rows the tenant-scoped store returned</b>,
       not in a GROUP BY built from the declaration. A declaration file that composes SQL can reach
       any table by any path, and a report is exactly the feature somebody adds in a hurry.</p>
    <p class="note">Each report sits behind the <b>view permission of the module that declares its
       table</b>, and a money report behind the salary permission as well &mdash; derived from what
       it reads rather than written on it, because a permission somebody has to remember to add to a
       new report is the one they forget. Reports you cannot open are listed and marked, not hidden:
       knowing a figure exists and that you may not see it is different from believing it does not
       exist.</p>
  </details>
<?php foreach ($reports->byGroup() as $groupName => $inGroup): ?>
