    <h2>Configuration</h2>
    <div class="problem">Denied: <?= e($access->decide((string) $session->user()['role'],
      'tenant.settings.view')->summary) ?></div>
    <p class="note">What a customer has changed about their own installation, and who changed it,
       is a settings record rather than a public one.</p>
  </div>

<?php elseif ($route === 'configuration'): ?>
<?php
    // Every key the application declares, so anything customised against a key
    // NOT in here can be named as an orphan rather than quietly listed.
    //
    // THE NON-MODULE SYSTEM KEYS belong here too. inventory() looks up a
    // customisation's row key AS SAVED — "employee-fields" for a module (no
    // colon, matches the bare module key below), but "access:role-
    // permissions", "app:branding" and "nav:sidebar" IN FULL for the three
    // system overrides, because none of those is a module, workflow, letter,
    // report or compliance check. Without an entry here for each, every one
    // of the three read as an orphan — "applies to nothing" — the moment it
    // was first saved, which is exactly the false "my settings vanished"
    // this screen exists to rule out, not cause.
    $known = [
        Authorisation::CUSTOMISATION_KEY => 'Roles & Permissions',
        'app:branding' => 'Application branding',
        'nav:sidebar' => 'Sidebar menu',
        // Hand-built configuration screens, keyed bare like a module (no
        // colon) but never declared in config/modules.json because they are
        // not the generic module engine — so $modules->all() below never
        // names them either.
        'employee-fields' => 'Employee field register',
    ];

    foreach ($modules->all() as $m) {
        $known[(string) $m['key']] = (string) $m['label'];
    }

    foreach ($requests->workflows() as $w) {
        $known['workflow:' . $w['key']] = (string) $w['label'];
    }

    foreach ($letters->all() as $l) {
        $known['letter:' . $l['key']] = (string) $l['label'];
    }

    foreach ($reports->all() as $rep) {
        $known['report:' . $rep['key']] = (string) $rep['label'];
    }

    foreach ($compliance->all() as $c) {
        $known['compliance:' . $c['key']] = (string) $c['label'];
    }

    $inventory = $customisation->inventory($known);
    $orphans = array_values(array_filter($inventory,
        static fn (array $r): bool => (bool) ($r['orphan'] ?? false)));
    $link = static fn (array $r): string => match ($r['kind']) {
        'workflow' => '?r=configure-workflow&k=' . rawurlencode((string) $r['key']),
        'letter' => '?r=configure-letter&k=' . rawurlencode((string) $r['key']),
        'report' => '?r=configure-report&k=' . rawurlencode((string) $r['key']),
        'compliance' => '?r=configure-check&k=' . rawurlencode((string) $r['key']),
        default => '?r=configure&k=' . rawurlencode((string) $r['key']),
    };
?>
  <div class="page-header">
    <div><h2>What is different about this installation</h2>
      <p><?= count($inventory) === 0
        ? 'Nothing has been customised — every screen, chain and letter is as it shipped.'
        : count($inventory) . ' thing(s) customised, out of ' . count($known)
          . ' that could be.' ?></p></div>
  </div>
  <details class="why"><summary>Why this screen exists</summary>
    <p class="note">A configuration-first product whose configuration is spread over three screens
       and a table has the same problem as a code-first one: nobody can answer &ldquo;what have we
       changed?&rdquo; without looking in four places. This is the fourth place, and it is one.</p>
  </details>

<?php if ($orphans !== []): ?>
  <div class="card">
    <div class="problem"><b><?= count($orphans) ?> customisation(s) apply to nothing.</b> The module,
      chain or template they were written against is no longer declared, so they sit here doing
      nothing &mdash; for ever &mdash; while whoever made the change believes it is in force. That
      is the quietest way this table can fail, which is why they are named rather than filtered out
      of the list below.</div>
    <table>
      <tr><th>Kind</th><th>Key</th><th>Last changed by</th><th>When</th></tr>
<?php foreach ($orphans as $row): ?>
      <tr><td><?= e((string) $row['kind']) ?></td>
        <td><code><?= e((string) $row['key']) ?></code></td>
        <td><?= e((string) $row['changed_by']) ?></td>
        <td><?= e((string) $row['at']) ?></td></tr>
<?php endforeach; ?>
    </table>
  </div>
<?php endif; ?>

<?php if ($inventory !== []): ?>
  <div class="card">
    <h2>Every change, and who made it</h2>
    <table>
      <tr><th>Kind</th><th>What</th><th>Live version</th><th>Kept</th><th>By</th><th>When</th>
        <th>Why</th></tr>
<?php foreach ($inventory as $row): ?>
      <tr>
        <td><?= e((string) $row['kind']) ?></td>
        <td><?= $row['orphan']
          ? '<code>' . e((string) $row['key']) . '</code> <span class="no">applies to nothing</span>'
          : '<a href="' . e($link($row)) . '">' . e((string) $row['label']) . '</a>' ?></td>
        <td class="n"><?= (int) $row['version'] ?></td>
        <td class="n note"><?= (int) $row['versions'] ?> version(s)</td>
        <td><?= e((string) $row['changed_by']) ?></td>
        <td class="note"><?= e((string) $row['at']) ?></td>
        <td class="note"><?= e((string) $row['note']) ?: 'not given' ?></td>
      </tr>
<?php endforeach; ?>
    </table>
    <p class="note">Nothing here was ever overwritten. Every earlier version is kept, because
       &ldquo;what did this say before somebody changed it?&rdquo; is asked precisely when something
       has gone wrong.</p>
  </div>
<?php endif; ?>

  <div class="card">
    <h2>The menu</h2>
    <p class="note">The sidebar is a declaration too &mdash; its headings, the words on each item,
       the icons and the order. In SmartPRS&nbsp;2 all of that is markup in a Blade template.</p>
    <p><a class="btn" href="?r=configure-menu">Change the menu →</a></p>
  </div>

  <div class="card">
    <h2>What can be changed, and what deliberately cannot</h2>
    <table>
      <tr><th>Declaration</th><th>Yours to change</th><th>Fixed, and why</th></tr>
      <tr><td><b><?= count($modules->all()) ?> modules</b><br>
          <span class="note"><code>config/modules.json</code></span></td>
        <td>the screen's name, the columns in the list and in the search box, and per field the
          label, input type, dropdown choices, help text, required, read-only, hidden</td>
        <td class="note">the table it reads and the permission in front of it &mdash; renaming a
          field must not repoint a screen or take a permission off it</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b><?= count($requests->workflows()) ?> approval chains</b><br>
          <span class="note"><code>config/workflows.json</code></span></td>
        <td>the amount each step joins at, the SLA, whether a reason is demanded, what a step is
          called</td>
        <td class="note">who decides a step &mdash; raising a threshold is doing the job, changing
          who signs is removing the second person the approval exists for</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b><?= count($letters->all()) ?> letter templates</b><br>
          <span class="note"><code>config/letters.json</code></span></td>
        <td>the name, the wording, and which fields must have a value before it will issue</td>
        <td class="note">the key the rest of the application finds it by; and a placeholder naming
          something no employee holds is refused, since it would never render for anybody</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b><?= count($reports->all()) ?> reports</b><br>
          <span class="note"><code>config/reports.json</code></span></td>
        <td>the name, the question it answers, the group it sits under, and which rows it counts</td>
        <td class="note">the table it reads and whether it is money &mdash; those decide the
          permission in front of the figure, since a report inherits the view permission of the
          module declaring its table and a money report the salary permission too. A form that could
          change either is a privilege escalation with a settings screen in front of it. A filter
          naming a column the table does not have is refused and the column named: it would match no
          rows, and an empty report reads as &ldquo;there is nothing to report&rdquo;</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b><?= count($compliance->all()) ?> compliance checks</b><br>
          <span class="note"><code>config/compliance.json</code></span></td>
        <td>the name, how many days' warning before a credential lapses, and &mdash; in words
          &mdash; what a lapse stops</td>
        <td class="note">the table, the date column and which rows the obligation is on &mdash;
          point a check at another column and every row reports as &ldquo;no date recorded&rdquo;,
          which reads as two hundred people who never submitted a date and is in fact one wrong
