      <p class="note">The control total is checked <b>against the run</b>, not recomputed from the
         rows just written — summing the file and comparing it to itself proves the addition, which
         was never in doubt. The question is whether the file agrees with the payroll it claims to
         pay. An employee with no bank details does not fall out of a filter: the advice refuses
         and names them, because a file that pays 47 of 48 people balances perfectly against its own
         total and is wrong.</p>
    </div>

<?php $corrections = $register->correctionsFor((string) $run['period']); ?>
<?php if ($may('payroll.run')): ?>
    <form method="post" style="margin-top:12px" id="correct-<?= e((string) $run['period']) ?>">
      <input type="hidden" name="csrf" value="<?= e($session->token()) ?>">
      <input type="hidden" name="action" value="correct-period">
      <input type="hidden" name="period" value="<?= e((string) $run['period']) ?>">
      <div class="row">
        <div style="flex:3"><label for="reason-<?= e((string) $run['period']) ?>">Raise a correction — why</label>
          <input id="reason-<?= e((string) $run['period']) ?>" name="reason"
            placeholder="Asha's 3 Sept punch was missing when the month closed"></div>
        <div style="display:flex;align-items:flex-end">
          <button type="submit">Recompute and record the difference</button></div>
      </div>
      <p class="note">This does <b>not</b> re-run the month. It recomputes from what the records say
         today and stores the <b>difference</b> alongside the original, which is left untouched.
         What was paid is history; what is owed is the only figure anyone acts on, and it is exactly
         what a correction that overwrites the original destroys. Refusing a re-run without offering
         this is how a payroll officer ends up editing the database.</p>
    </form>
<?php endif; ?>

<?php foreach ($corrections as $correction): ?>
