  <details class="why"><summary>Why a scope, and why the token is never shown twice</summary>
    <p class="note">A credential carries <b>scopes</b>, not a role. A scope is a coarser vocabulary
       than the permission catalogue on purpose: the blast radius of a token has to be statable in
       one line to whoever is issuing it, and "this key can read employees" is a sentence a person
       can weigh. An endpoint whose scope a credential does not hold is refused even when the code
       path behind it would have worked.</p>
    <p class="note"><b>The token is stored as a hash.</b> The list below can show you a
       credential's name, prefix, scopes, expiry and when it was last used &mdash; and never the
       token, because it is not there to show. That is what makes a revoke meaningful: there is no
       copy anywhere for somebody to keep using.</p>
    <p class="note"><code>last_used_at</code> is stamped on every authenticated request, which is
       what makes &ldquo;this key has not been used since March&rdquo; answerable &mdash; and that
       question is the one that makes revoking an old key safe to do.</p>
  </details>

