  <div class="page-header">
    <div><h2><?= $mIsLogs ? 'Attendance logs' : 'Attendance report' ?></h2>
      <p><?= $mIsLogs
        ? 'Every employee, every day, with the punches behind it &mdash; from the same settlement the register pays from'
        : 'Attendance status &mdash; every employee, every day, from the same settlement the register pays from' ?></p></div>
    <?= attendanceSwitch($mIsLogs ? 'daily-attendance' : 'attendance-report', '&amp;from=' . e($mFrom) . '&amp;to=' . e($mTo)) ?>
  </div>
  <details class="why"><summary>Why this report cannot disagree with the payslip</summary>
    <p class="note">Every cell is <code>Attendance::settledDays()</code> &mdash; the same
       <code>DaySettlement</code> and the same published rules (<code>config/attendance-rules.json</code>)
       that decide what a day is worth on the payslip. SmartPRS&nbsp;2's report pairs the punches a
       second time, judges lateness against a second shift written into the controller
       (<code>09:30</code>&ndash;<code>18:30</code>), and <b>generates demo punches for any day in
       range with no logs</b> &mdash; into the table payroll reads. None of that exists here. A day
       with no punches is a cell that says ABSENT, WEEKLY_OFF or HOLIDAY because the rule engine
       said so, ON_LEAVE because an approved request covers it, or blank because it has not
       happened yet.</p>
  </details>

  <div class="card">
    <?= orgFilterBar('attendance-report', $mFilter, $mOptions, $mView === 'daily' ? ['v' => 'daily'] : [], 'Show', null, $mPeriod, ['month', 'year', 'mtd', 'week', 'custom']) ?>
    <div class="note">Shift <?= e($calendar->shiftStart()) ?>&ndash;<?= e($calendar->shiftEnd()) ?> from the working calendar &middot; up to <?= (int) round($mColumns['max_range_days'] / 30.44) ?> months at a time</div>
