    // ones, and the times did not line up down the column. `"align": "right"`
    // now lives beside each column's own label in config/attendance-report.json,
    // which is where a tenant's columns are already described (Law 1), and a
    // column added there arrives aligned without this file changing.
    $mRight = static fn (string $c): bool
        => (string) ($mColumns['declared'][$c]['align'] ?? '') === 'right';
?>
  <style>
    /* ONE EMPLOYEE-DAY, ONE ROW, ON ONE LINE (owner, 23 Sep: "the columns are
       still not aligned and not formatted properly"). Twenty columns at the
       stock 9px/10px padding leaves every text cell too narrow for its own
       content, so "Karthik Kumar Joshi" became three lines, "2026-09-01" broke
       at its hyphens, and each row grew to sixty pixels of ragged, top-aligned
       text. Nothing here drops or shortens a value: it stops them WRAPPING,
       pays a tighter padding rate, and centres each cell against its row.

       LONG VALUES ARE CLIPPED, NEVER WRAPPED. A branch called "EON IT Park,
       Madhapur" gets an ellipsis and carries the whole string in its title, so
       it is one hover away and complete in the CSV export. That is the same
       bargain the leave approvals table makes, and it is only honest because
       both halves of it are true.

       TWENTY COLUMNS STILL DO NOT FIT A LAPTOP — that is arithmetic, not
       styling, and the lever for it is Columns, which is already on this
       screen. This makes the table as narrow as it can honestly be. */
    .att-table th,.att-table td{white-space:nowrap;padding:5px 8px;vertical-align:middle}
    .att-table td{font-size:12.5px}
    .att-table th{font-size:11px;padding-top:7px;padding-bottom:7px}
