      <tbody>
<?php
  // THE HEADS IN FORCE, ASKED OF THE PACKS. This said `compute('PT', ...)`, and
  // the phase 5 exit gate had been failing on it: a component code written into
  // a screen is law 1's business literal, and the moment the name is the
  // behaviour, renaming it changes the arithmetic. Worse than the principle is
  // what it did — the page showed exactly one head for ever, so the day a real
  // pack brings PF, ESI and TDS this screen would still have shown professional
  // tax and nothing would have said the others existed.
  //
  // No heads is a NAMED state, not an empty table: "nothing is declared" and
  // "nothing was computed" look identical on a blank screen and mean opposite
  // things to somebody deciding whether payroll is safe to run.
  $probeDates = [date('Y-m-t') => 'the period being computed',
      '2026-10-31' => 'a date the synthetic pack is in force'];
  // ACROSS EVERY DATE PROBED, not just today. The first version asked for the
  // heads in force on the period end alone — and the second probe date exists
  // precisely BECAUSE the synthetic pack is in force then and not now, so the
  // table went empty and reported "no head is declared" while the row below it
  // would have had something to say. A screen that asks about two dates has to
  // ask both of them what exists.
  $inForce = [];

  foreach (array_keys($probeDates) as $probe) {
      foreach ($statutory->heads(new DateTimeImmutable((string) $probe)) as $declared) {
          $inForce[$declared] = true;
      }
  }

  $inForce = array_keys($inForce);
  sort($inForce);
?>
<?php if ($inForce === []): ?>
      <tr><td colspan="3"><span class="badge badge-red">no statutory head is in force</span>
        <span class="note">Not "nothing is deducted" — nothing is DECLARED. No pack in force on
        this date names a head, so there is nothing for the engine to compute and no figure
        below is missing.</span></td></tr>
<?php endif; ?>
<?php foreach ($inForce as $head): ?>
<?php foreach ($probeDates as $asOf => $label): ?>
<?php $computed = $statutory->compute($head, Employment::code($employment),
        new DateTimeImmutable((string) $asOf), $amounts, $flags); ?>
      <tr>
        <td><b><?= e($head) ?></b><br><?= e((string) $asOf) ?><br><span class="note"><?= e($label) ?></span></td>
        <td class="n"><b><?php if ($computed['row'] === null): ?><span
            class="badge badge-red">refused</span>
          <?php elseif (!$computed['row']->applicable): ?><span
            class="badge badge-red">not determined</span>
          <?php else: ?><span style="font-family:var(--mono)"><?=
            e($computed['row']->amount->format()) ?></span><?php endif; ?></b></td>
        <td class="note"><?php if ($computed['row'] === null): ?>
          <?= e((string) $computed['refusal']) ?>
        <?php else: ?><pre style="margin:0;white-space:pre-wrap;font-size:11px"><?=
          e($computed['row']->explain()) ?></pre>
          <?php if (!$computed['row']->applicable): ?><b class="no">This is NOT a zero deduction.</b>
          Nothing was determined for this date, and the row says so — an undeclared statute is not
          &ldquo;does not apply&rdquo;, it is a question nobody asked.<?php endif; ?>
        <?php endif; ?></td>
      </tr>
<?php endforeach; ?>
<?php endforeach; ?>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    </div>
    <p class="note">The wage base is the sum of components carrying the <b>declared flag</b> the
       rule names — <code>is_synth_base</code>, added to <code>C-BASIC</code> in
       <code>config/salary-structure.json</code>. Never a list of component codes: in SmartPRS 2 the
       PF base is whichever components a controller happens to enumerate, so renaming a component
       silently changes what it contributes to. A statutory scheme this build has not met yet is a
       flag somebody adds to the configuration, not a release.</p>
    <p class="note">A statute that applies and cannot be computed is a <b>refusal</b>, never a zero.
       A zero deduction that should have been a real one looks exactly like a correct one on the
       payslip, and the employee finds out at assessment. Every figure carries its rule code, pack
       version, content hash and source reference, so it can be re-derived in 2031 — a figure nobody
       can re-derive is not a figure, it is a claim.</p>
  </div>

